SECURITY RECEIPTS — This case is fantastic for it
What Instructure initially established

There had been unauthorized access to Canvas information.

Certain student and user information was involved.

Core learning content, submissions and credentials were not believed compromised.

What became clearer later

Instructure identified the exploited area as the Free-for-Teacher environment, with CEO Steve Daly specifically referencing a vulnerability involving support tickets.

The incident was also not merely passive theft. Attackers later modified pages seen by users at multiple schools, creating an operational and integrity dimension in addition to confidentiality loss.

What the threat actor claimed

ShinyHunters claimed:

nearly 9,000 institutions
approximately 275 million individuals
multi-terabyte data theft

These claims should remain separated from company-confirmed scope.

What changed operationally

The Free-for-Teacher product wasn't merely patched and restored.

It was ultimately permanently discontinued.

That is significant.

InfinityGrid should explicitly call this out:

The affected trust surface was ultimately removed from production rather than simply returned to its previous operating state.

That tells readers the incident appears to have triggered a material product/security architecture decision.
